`make rust-test` is RED on ONE test and the branch was NOT merged because of it:

  crates/kuna-decomp/tests/verify_w10_proto_unlock.rs:202
  w10_proto_unlock_const_return_collapses_no_tied_roundtrip
  "promote_compare still emits a tied-register constant round-trip
   (`reg = N;` then `return reg;`) -- the all_writes_const_copy un-tie did not fire"

That test drives decomp_test_dbg end to end on the i386 `promotecompare`
datatest and asserts the constant returns collapse to literal `return 0;` /
`return 1;`. It passed on the parent commit, so this branch causes it.

The likely mechanism, for whoever picks this up: `mark_output_storage_addr_tied`
(coreaction_cleanup.rs) leaves the return register un-tied only when EVERY write
to it is a `CPUI_COPY` of a constant. With `calleeretpreserves` on, a call in
that function no longer plants an INDIRECT creation of the return register, so
the set of writes reaching the RET has changed shape and the `all_writes_const_copy`
predicate no longer holds. The two candidate fixes, in order of preference:

  1. Teach `all_writes_const_copy` about the write set it now sees (the correct
     fix if the collapse is still valid on the new dataflow).
  2. If it is not, the datatest expectation is the thing that moved and the
     change is a behaviour change on i386 that needs its own DIV justification --
     i.e. reconsider whether the rule should apply where the caller's surviving
     definition is a bare constant COPY.

Everything else in this branch is finished: the option, its three evidence
gates, the fastfail-aware probe, the phases.toml row and every hard-coded
counter, docs/options.md, the DIV-133 row, the spec chapter, the two-pass stage
test with its endbr64/ret negative control, the vendored
pe_cookiecheck_x86_64.exe fixture and its generator, and the promoted
tests/cli/main-returns-invented-cookie.json.
